    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm p-8 md:p-12 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          You bought a smart deadbolt, or you're about to, and now the box is sitting on the counter. Smart lock installation in Dover, DE is a job many homeowners can do alone with a screwdriver. It also goes wrong in a few predictable ways, and the door is often the culprit, not the lock. If the door is level, the holes are standard, and you pick a lock with a key backup, a first-timer can often finish the same afternoon. If not, you'll save yourself a bad afternoon by calling someone.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Here is how to tell which camp you're in, what to check before you open the box, and how to keep the lock safe once it's on.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-2">Key Takeaways</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-gray-700 space-y-1 mb-0">
            <li>Check the door first. A door that is out of level can stop a new smart lock from latching, and it derailed Security.org's own test install.</li>
            <li>A retrofit lock swaps only the inside half and keeps your existing deadbolt and key.</li>
            <li>Pick a lock with a physical key or an outside battery terminal as backup.</li>
            <li>Set a strong code, turn on two-step sign-in, and keep the firmware current.</li>
          </ul>
        </blockquote>

        <nav aria-label="Table of contents" class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-10 not-prose">
          <p class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">In This Article</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-[#A61E22] space-y-1 text-sm">
