        </nav>

        <h2 id="what-is-a-smart-lock-and-what-does-installing-one-involve" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Is a Smart Lock, and What Does Installing One Involve?</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">What is a smart lock?</strong> A smart lock is an electronic deadbolt or deadbolt add-on that opens with a code, phone, or fingerprint instead of, or alongside, a key. It runs on batteries and usually connects to an app by Bluetooth or Wi-Fi.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Installing one means removing your old deadbolt, or its inside half, and fitting the new hardware in the same door holes. The steps are about the same for most brands. <a href="https://www.security.org/smart-locks/install/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Security.org</a> lists them for a typical retrofit install: ready the existing deadbolt, remove the thumb latch, attach the mounting plate and adapter, attach the new lock, take off the battery tab, and test the lock. After that you create the app account, name the lock, and connect it to Wi-Fi or a hub.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          That sounds easy, and the lock part often is. The same guide also calls smart locks some of the hardest smart home products to install, and notes that a problem with the door can turn a 30-minute job into a full day.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-4-check-door-test-before-you-buy" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The 4-Check Door Test Before You Buy</h2>

        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-[#A61E22] bg-[#F8F8F5] px-6 py-4 my-8 not-italic">
          <p class="text-gray-700 mb-0"><strong class="text-[#1A1F22]">What is the 4-Check Door Test?</strong> The 4-Check Door Test is a short walk around your own front door before you buy a smart lock. You check the hole size, the backset, the lock type, and whether the door closes level. If all four pass, a DIY install is a fair bet.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Do this with the door open, a tape measure, and five minutes.
        </p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Measure the large hole.</strong> The big hole through the door face is called the cross bore. <a href="https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/cross-bore-edge-bore-and-backset" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allegion</a>, a lock maker, describes the standard prep as 2-1/8 inches, with a 1-inch hole in the door edge for the latch. Most new smart locks expect this.</li>
          <li><strong>Measure the backset.</strong> That's the distance from the door edge to the center of the big hole. Standard sizes are 2-3/8 inches and 2-3/4 inches. Many locks fit either one, but check the box.</li>
          <li><strong>Know what you have now.</strong> Some smart locks need a single-cylinder deadbolt, meaning a keyway outside and a thumb turn inside. If your current lock has keyways on both sides, say so before you buy.</li>
          <li><strong>Close the door and watch the bolt.</strong> Throw the existing deadbolt. If it drags, rubs the frame, or has to be lifted to line up, the door is out of level. A smart lock will not fix that. It will make it worse, because a motor can't force a bolt into a hole it can't find.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Check four is the one that matters most, because no lock can fix it. If you've been fighting a sticky deadbolt already, our post on <a href="/blog/lock-repair-vs-replace-dover-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">repairing or replacing a door lock</a> walks through the door-open test that finds the cause.
        </p>

        <h2 id="retrofit-lock-or-full-smart-deadbolt" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Retrofit Lock or Full Smart Deadbolt?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A retrofit smart lock replaces only the inside part of your deadbolt and keeps the outside lock and key. A full smart deadbolt replaces the whole lock. Retrofit is simpler and keeps your key. A full replacement gives you a keypad on the outside of the door.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Retrofit locks have gone mainstream. <a href="https://www.consumerreports.org/home-garden/door-locks/best-smart-locks-of-the-year-a3383819474/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Consumer Reports</a> says three of its ten top smart lock picks are retrofit models, which replace only the part of the lock on the interior side of the door. Because the outside deadbolt stays put, you can still use a regular key.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The two compare like this.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200">
            <thead class="bg-[#1A1F22] text-white">
              <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold"></th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Retrofit smart lock</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Full smart deadbolt</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">What changes</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Inside half only</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The whole lock</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Outside of the door</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Looks the same</td><td class="px-4 py-3">New keypad or touchscreen</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Your existing key</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Keeps working</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Replaced by a new key set</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Good fit when</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Your current deadbolt is solid and you want phone control</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You want a keypad, or the old lock is worn</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Main risk</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Depends on your old lock being in good shape</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Door must match the new lock's hole sizes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A retrofit won't help a worn or low-quality deadbolt. If the lock underneath is the weak point, a full swap makes more sense. Our <a href="/services/deadbolt-installation/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">deadbolt installation</a> and <a href="/services/lock-change/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock change</a> services cover that.
        </p>

        <h2 id="diy-or-pro-a-quick-decision-table" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">DIY or Pro? A Quick Decision Table</h2>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200">
            <thead class="bg-[#1A1F22] text-white">
              <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Your situation</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Best route</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Why</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Level door, standard holes, single-cylinder deadbolt</td><td class="px-4 py-3">DIY</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The job is mostly swapping parts in place</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Door rubs or the bolt needs a lift to line up</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Pro</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The door needs adjusting before any lock goes on</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Old lock has keyways on both sides</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Pro</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You need a compatible lock and a clean swap</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Holes are the wrong size or have been drilled twice</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Pro</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Door prep takes tools and a steady hand</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Lock that must be wired in</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Pro</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Security.org says smart locks are hardest to install when they must be hardwired</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">New lock for a rental or a home you're moving into</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Pro, with rekeying</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Old keys should stop working at the same visit</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you've just bought or moved into a house, think about the keys before the app. A smart lock does not cancel keys already floating around. Pairing it with <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock rekeying</a> makes sure the old ones stop working.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-actually-goes-wrong-with-a-smart-lock-installation" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Actually Goes Wrong With a Smart Lock Installation?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most smart lock problems are not hacking. They are dead batteries, a door that won't latch, weak Wi-Fi, and pairing that drops after a battery change. Plan for each one before you install.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <strong>A door that won't latch.</strong> The Security.org testers' own first install failed because the door wasn't level with the frame. Wood doors move with the seasons, so a door that closed fine in July can start to rub by January.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <strong>Batteries.</strong> Most consumer smart locks run on non-rechargeable batteries, and Security.org advises keeping spares on hand. Buy a lock with a physical key or an outside terminal where you can touch a battery to the lock for a jump start. A lock with neither turns a dead battery into a lockout. Once you have the key, test it twice a year, because a cylinder that rarely turns can seize and you don't want to find that out in the dark.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <strong>Wi-Fi only.</strong> A lock that depends on Wi-Fi alone can fail when your connection does, so look for Bluetooth, a keypad, or a key as a second way in.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <strong>Pairing after a battery swap.</strong> The app connection can drop after new batteries go in, so check the maker's reset steps before you need them.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We'd rather you read that list now than learn it standing on the porch. Locked out anyway? A <a href="/services/house-lockout/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">house lockout</a> call is what we do.
        </p>

        <h2 id="are-smart-locks-safe" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Are Smart Locks Safe?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A reputable smart lock that is set up well is a sound choice for most homes. The real risks are weak codes, outdated firmware, and shared access you forget to turn off.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Skepticism about smart locks is fair. A smart lock is only as strong as the deadbolt and door it's mounted on, so a solid door and a good strike plate matter more than the app. Delaware's Statistical Analysis Center counted 301 reported burglaries in Kent County in 2024, and 149 of them were unlawful entries with no force (<a href="https://sac.delaware.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/64/2026/01/CiD-20-24-Main-Report-final-010726.pdf" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Crime in Delaware, 2020-2024</a>). That's about half of them.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          There are real documented weaknesses, too. The federal CERT Coordination Center published an advisory (VU#949046) on locks that use Sciener firmware and the TTLock app, sold under many brand names. It found issues that let an attacker within Bluetooth range compromise a lock, and says there is no software fix, only a workaround of turning off some Bluetooth features (<a href="https://www.kb.cert.org/vuls/id/949046" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CERT/CC</a>). The takeaway: buy from a maker that supports its product and posts firmware updates.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A short habit list covers most of the risk:
        </p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Use a code that isn't a birthday or an address.</li>
          <li>Turn on two-step sign-in for the lock's app account.</li>
          <li>Install firmware updates when the app offers them.</li>
          <li>Delete codes for guests and workers when they're no longer needed.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="who-handles-smart-lock-installation-in-dover-smyrna-and-kent-county" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Who Handles Smart Lock Installation in Dover, Smyrna and Kent County?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We install and service locks across Dover, Camden, Smyrna, Felton, Harrington, Magnolia, Frederica, Cheswold, Clayton, Milford and Leipsic, and we come to you. Our <a href="/services/residential-locksmith/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">residential locksmith services</a> cover the whole area. A door that has settled over the years is exactly the kind that trips up a DIY smart lock.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you're in Smyrna and want a smart lock fitted the day you move in, start with our <a href="/service-areas/smyrna-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Smyrna service page</a> or call us. We'd rather talk through your door over the phone than have you buy a lock that doesn't fit.
        </p>

        <h2 id="should-you-install-a-smart-lock-yourself-or-call-a-pro" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Should You Install a Smart Lock Yourself or Call a Pro?</h2>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Do the 4-Check Door Test. If the door is level and the holes are standard, smart lock installation is a fair weekend project with a retrofit lock. If the door fights you, or you'd rather skip the guesswork, call a locksmith who can fix the door and fit the lock in one visit.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Not sure which camp you're in? Call 1st Capital Locksmith at (302) 735-0050 or <a href="/contact/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">request a visit</a>, and tell us what door you have. We're <a href="/about/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">locally owned and operated</a>, bonded and insured, and we'll explain what needs doing before we start.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-2">Sources</h3>
