        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 not-prose">
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Can I install a smart lock myself?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Often, yes. If your door is level, the holes are standard size, and your current deadbolt is a single-cylinder type, many people finish the same afternoon. If the door rubs or the holes need work, a locksmith will save you time.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              What size hole does a smart lock need?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most expect a 2-1/8 inch hole through the door and a 1-inch hole in the edge, with a backset of 2-3/8 or 2-3/4 inches, according to Allegion. Measure yours and check the box before you buy.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              What is a retrofit smart lock?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A retrofit smart lock replaces only the inside part of your deadbolt. The outside lock stays, so your key still works. Consumer Reports counts three retrofit models among its ten top picks.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Are smart locks safe?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For most homes, yes, if the brand is reputable and you set it up well. Use a strong code, turn on two-step sign-in, install firmware updates, and delete old guest codes.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              What happens if the battery dies?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Pick a lock with a physical key or an outside terminal for a jump start. Replace batteries when the app warns you, and keep spares at home.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              Do I need Wi-Fi for a smart lock?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Not always. Many locks work over Bluetooth or a keypad with no Wi-Fi. Wi-Fi adds remote control, but a Wi-Fi-only lock can fail when your internet does.</p>
          </details>
        </div>

